{"subjectId":"python","id":"context-managers","title":"Context Managers & contextlib","group":"Advanced Python","order":42,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A context manager pairs setup with guaranteed teardown. <code>with</code> runs the teardown however the block exits — normally, by <code>return</code>, or by an exception.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug-connected\"></i>The protocol</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Resource:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(f\"open {self.name}\")\n        return self                          # bound to the \"as\" target\n    def __exit__(self, exc_type, exc, tb):\n        print(f\"close {self.name} (error: {exc_type.__name__ if exc_type else None})\")\n        return False                         # False/None → let the exception propagate\n\nwith Resource(\"db\") as r:\n    print(\"using\", r.name)\n\ntry:\n    with Resource(\"file\"):\n        raise KeyError(\"boom\")\nexcept KeyError:\n    print(\"caught outside\")</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>open db\nusing db\nclose db (error: None)\nopen file\nclose file (error: KeyError)\ncaught outside</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Evaluate</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The expression after <code>with</code> produces the manager.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Enter</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>__enter__()</code> runs; its return value is bound to the <code>as</code> name.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Body</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The indented block runs.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Exit</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>__exit__(exc_type, exc, tb)</code> always runs — all three are <code>None</code> if there was no exception.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">5</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Suppress?</div><div class=\"step-desc\">If <code>__exit__</code> returns a truthy value, the exception is swallowed. Almost always return <code>False</code>.</div></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>A reusable class-based manager</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import time\n\nclass Timer:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.start = time.perf_counter()\n        return self\n    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n        self.elapsed = time.perf_counter() - self.start\n        return False\n\nwith Timer() as t:\n    sum(range(100_000))\nprint(t.elapsed &gt; 0)          # True</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i><code>@contextmanager</code>: a generator becomes a manager</h3>\n<p>Everything before <code>yield</code> is <code>__enter__</code>, the yielded value is the <code>as</code> target, everything after is <code>__exit__</code>. Wrap the <code>yield</code> in <code>try</code>/<code>finally</code> or the cleanup is skipped when the body raises.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef temporary_setting(config, key, value):\n    old = config[key]\n    config[key] = value\n    try:\n        yield config\n    finally:\n        config[key] = old             # restored even if the body raises\n\ncfg = {\"debug\": False}\nwith temporary_setting(cfg, \"debug\", True):\n    print(cfg[\"debug\"])               # True\nprint(cfg[\"debug\"])                   # False</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>contextlib helpers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import io\nfrom contextlib import suppress, redirect_stdout, nullcontext, closing\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):                  # ignore one specific exception\n    {}[\"missing\"]\n\nbuf = io.StringIO()\nwith redirect_stdout(buf):                # capture print output\n    print(\"hidden\")\nprint(buf.getvalue().strip())             # hidden\n\ndef process(lock=None):\n    with lock or nullcontext():           # \"maybe a lock\" without an if/else\n        return \"processed\"\nprint(process())                          # processed\n\nclass Conn:\n    def close(self):\n        self.closed = True\nwith closing(Conn()) as c:                # calls c.close() on exit\n    pass\nprint(c.closed)                           # True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Helper</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>suppress(*excs)</code></td><td>Ignore the listed exceptions inside the block</td></tr><tr><td><code>redirect_stdout(f)</code> / <code>redirect_stderr(f)</code></td><td>Temporarily send prints somewhere else</td></tr><tr><td><code>closing(obj)</code></td><td>Call <code>obj.close()</code> on exit, for objects without <code>__exit__</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>nullcontext(value)</code></td><td>A do-nothing manager — for optional context</td></tr><tr><td><code>ExitStack()</code></td><td>Enter a <em>dynamic</em> number of managers; unwinds them in reverse</td></tr><tr><td><code>chdir(path)</code></td><td>Temporarily change the working directory (3.11+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ContextDecorator</code></td><td>A manager that can also be used as <code>@decorator</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>asynccontextmanager</code></td><td>The <code>async with</code> version of <code>@contextmanager</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>ExitStack: many resources at once</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom contextlib import ExitStack\n\nfolder = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())\nnames = [\"a.txt\", \"b.txt\", \"c.txt\"]\nwith ExitStack() as stack:\n    files = [stack.enter_context(open(folder / n, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\")) for n in names]\n    for i, f in enumerate(files):\n        f.write(str(i))\n# every file is closed here, even if one open() had failed half-way\nprint(all(f.closed for f in files), (folder / \"c.txt\").read_text())   # True 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Several managers in one <code>with</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import io\n\nsrc, dst = io.StringIO(\"copy me\"), io.StringIO()\nwith (\n    src as reader,          # parenthesised form (3.10+) allows one per line\n    dst as writer,\n):\n    writer.write(reader.read())\n    copied = writer.getvalue()\nprint(copied, src.closed, dst.closed)   # copy me True True</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Async context managers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import asyncio\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def session(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)          # e.g. open a network connection\n    try:\n        yield f\"session:{name}\"\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)      # e.g. close it\n\nasync def main():\n    async with session(\"api\") as s:\n        return s\n\nprint(asyncio.run(main()))          # session:api</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>with</code> for anything that must be released: files, locks, DB transactions, network connections, temporary state.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Returning <code>True</code> from <code>__exit__</code> \"just in case\" — it silently swallows every exception raised in the block.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if an exception occurs inside a with block?</summary><div><p><code>__exit__</code> is called with the exception type, value and traceback. If it returns a falsy value the exception continues propagating after cleanup; if it returns <code>True</code> the exception is suppressed and execution continues after the <code>with</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you write a context manager without a class?</summary><div><p>Decorate a generator with <code>contextlib.contextmanager</code>: setup, <code>yield</code> the resource inside <code>try</code>, clean up in <code>finally</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use ExitStack?</summary><div><p>When the number of resources is only known at runtime (open N files), or when you need to conditionally enter managers or register arbitrary cleanup callbacks with <code>stack.callback(func)</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}